  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "K. Reghu vs Food Inspector on 4 June, 2019 Author: V Raja Vijayaraghavan Bench: V Raja Vijayaraghavan In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present The Honourable Mr. Justice Raja Vijayaraghavan V Tuesday, The 04Th Day Of June 2019 / 14Th Jyaishta, 1941 Crl.MC.No. 7829 of 2016 St 595/2009 of Additional Chief Judicial Magistrate (Economic Offences), Ernakulam Petitioner/Accused No.1: K. Reghu, Aged 50 Years, S/O.Late V N Krishnan, Now Residing At 'Prarthana', Thekkumbhagom, Thripoonithura, Ernakulam, By Adv. Sri.Sivaram Respondent/Complainant: 1 Food Inspector, Corporation Of Cochin, Ernakulam, Kochi-682011. 2 State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala,Ernakulam-682031. By Sri.M.S.Breez, Senior Public Prosecutor",
    "facts": "The petitioner is the 1st accused in S.T.No.596 of 2009 pending on the files of the Additional Chief Judicial Magistrate (Economic Offences) Court, Ernakulam. In the aforesaid case, he is being proceeded against for having committed offences punishable under Sections 2(ia), 7(i)(v) and read with Section 16 (1) (a) (ii) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 (hereinafter referred to as \"the PFA Act\" for short) and read with Rule 29 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1955 (hereinafter referred to as \"the PFA Rules\" for short). 2. The prosecution allegation is that on 16.03.2009 at about 12.00 noon, the Food Inspector visited the supermarket, where the petitioner was working as the Manager of the enterprises, and collected samples of Banana Wafers, which were kept in the shop for sale to the public. The seized items were sampled and labelled in accordance with law and one part of the sample was forwarded to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst, in his report dated 18.04.2009, came to the conclusion that the sample contains added synthetic Food Colour Tartrazine (Colour Index 19140) which is in contravention of Rule 29 of the PFA Rules and is, therefore, adulterated. The said analysis report is produced as Annexure-2. Thereafter, based on the said report, Annexure-1 complaint was preferred before the learned Magistrate.",
    "arguments": "4. The contention of the learned counsel appearing for the petitioner revolves on the interpretation of Sections 23(1A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act. It is submitted that relying on the ratio of the judgment of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings Pvt. Ltd. v. Food Inspector and another [2010 (4) KLT 706 (SC)], a Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed N.K. V Food Inspector, Thalasserry (2016 (1) KLD 704) has laid down that Sections 23(1A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act are mandatory and any prosecution instituted under the Act, in violation of the said provisions, will have to necessarily bite the dust. The Division Bench in Rasheed N.K. (supra), after interpreting the various provisions of the PFA Act, 1957, in line with the pronouncement of the Apex Court in Pepsico Case (supra), has held that in order for the Analyst report to become the basis for launching a prosecution, the analysis of the food has to be conducted in a laboratory defined under S.23(1A)(ee) of the Act. It has been held in Rasheed N.K. (supra) that till the K. Reghu vs Food Inspector on 4 June, 2019 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/125119879/ 2 repealing of PFA Act, 1954, no laboratory had been defined in terms of the provision under S.23(1A)(ee).",
    "petitioner_arguments": "4. The contention of the learned counsel appearing for the petitioner revolves on the interpretation of Sections 23(1A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act. It has been held in Rasheed N.K. (supra) that till the K. Reghu vs Food Inspector on 4 June, 2019 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/125119879/ 2 repealing of PFA Act, 1954, no laboratory had been defined in terms of the provision under S.23(1A)(ee).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It is submitted that relying on the ratio of the judgment of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings Pvt. Ltd. v. Food Inspector and another [2010 (4) KLT 706 (SC)], a Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed N.K. V Food Inspector, Thalasserry (2016 (1) KLD 704) has laid down that Sections 23(1A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act are mandatory and any prosecution instituted under the Act, in violation of the said provisions, will have to necessarily bite the dust. The Division Bench in Rasheed N.K. (supra), after interpreting the various provisions of the PFA Act, 1957, in line with the pronouncement of the Apex Court in Pepsico Case (supra), has held that in order for the Analyst report to become the basis for launching a prosecution, the analysis of the food has to be conducted in a laboratory defined under S.23(1A)(ee) of the Act."
  },
